it was almost like Carnival the Portilla  Samba School in Rio de Janeiro was the  latest stop for presidential candidate  luisi nasso Lula da Silva campaigning  for another crack at the presidency  not that his supporters needed much  convincing  thousands of mostly low-income  Brazilians were nearly crushed trying to  get a glimpse of the candidate using  their fingers to make the letter L for  Lula others were moved to tears when  Lula spoke of how he too knew what it  was like to grow up in abject poverty  I just want to be president to take care  of my people because I know what people  want they want a house no matter how  small to Shelter From the rain and the  sun they want a job with a fair salary  the biggest dream of many women here is  not to leave their children a fortune  but rather a profession so they can rest  in peace and go to heaven  although Lula is leading in the polls he  warned the enthusiastic crowd that  former U.S presidential candidate  Hillary Clinton lost the election  because too many Americans stayed home  instead of voting Luna compared himself  to Gandhi and Mandela whom he said like  himself had been unfairly imprisoned but  eventually got out to free their country  but unlike Gandhi and Mandela charges  that he had overseen the largest  corruption scandal in Brazilian history  while he was President billions and  billions of dollars continue to weigh  heavily on Lula but people like Juliana  believe his virtues are more important  than his faults  it was Lula's government that gave  people opportunities like the chance for  the poorest sector of society to go to  university I simply cannot defend the  current president's policies that's why  we need Lula  this campaign event took place in what  is supposed to be the stronghold of his  rival Jade bolsonaro who is fighting for  a second term as president that Lula is  even running it all is something that  would have seemed Unthinkable four years  ago when he was sent to prison on  corruption charges something he always  denied  but then he always promised he would be  back  Lucia Newman Al Jazeera Rio de Janeiro
 

